{"slug":"tower-of-hanoi","title":"Tower of Hanoi","topic":"basics","difficulty":"hard","order":86,"tags":["recursion","divide-and-conquer"],"timeLimitMs":2000,"concept":{"title":"Trust the smaller problem","body":["There are three rods and `n` disks of different sizes stacked on rod 1, the biggest at the bottom. You may move one disk at a time from the top of one rod to another, and you may never put a bigger disk on a smaller one. The goal is to move the whole stack to rod 3.","Thinking about the individual moves is hopeless. Think recursively instead: **assume you already know how to move a stack of `n - 1` disks**. Then moving `n` disks is easy:",{"list":["move the top `n - 1` disks from rod 1 to the spare rod 2 (the recursive call),","move the biggest disk from rod 1 to rod 3 (one move),","move the `n - 1` disks from rod 2 on top of it, to rod 3 (the recursive call again)."]},"The base case is a stack of 0 disks: nothing to do. The function needs the roles of the rods as parameters: `hanoi(k, source, target, spare)`. In the second recursive call the roles change, because the old spare rod is now the source.","The number of moves `M(n)` satisfies `M(n) = 2 * M(n-1) + 1`, which gives `2^n - 1`. Each extra disk doubles the work. That is why the legend says the world ends when monks finish moving 64 disks: 2^64 moves is far beyond what any computer can do."]},"statement":["Given `n`, print the minimal sequence of moves that transfers `n` disks from rod 1 to rod 3, using rod 2 as spare.","First print the number of moves. Then print the moves, one per line, as two numbers `from to`. Use the standard recursive solution: first move `n - 1` disks from the source to the spare rod, then the biggest disk, then the `n - 1` disks from the spare rod to the target."],"inputFormat":["A single integer `n`."],"outputFormat":["The number of moves `2^n - 1` on the first line, then that many lines `from to`."],"constraints":["1 <= n <= 10"],"examples":[{"input":"2\n","output":"3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n","note":"Three moves: disk to rod 2, disk to rod 3, then the small disk onto it."},{"input":"1\n","output":"1\n1 3\n","note":"One move from rod 1 to rod 3."}],"hints":["Write a function `hanoi(k, source, target, spare)` that prints the moves for `k` disks. For `k = 0` it does nothing.","It calls itself for `k - 1` disks (source to spare), prints the move `source target`, and calls itself again for `k - 1` disks (spare to target).","Before the first call print the number of moves, `2^n - 1`."],"starter":{"python":"def hanoi(k, source, target, spare):\n    # Print the moves for k disks\n    pass\n\nn = int(input())\n\nprint(2 ** n - 1)\nhanoi(n, 1, 3, 2)\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid hanoi(int k, int source, int target, int spare) {\n    // Print the moves for k disks\n}\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    cout << (1LL << n) - 1 << \"\\n\";\n    hanoi(n, 1, 3, 2);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void hanoi(int k, int source, int target, int spare) {\n        // Print the moves for k disks\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        System.out.println((1L << n) - 1);\n        hanoi((int) n, 1, 3, 2);\n    }\n}\n"},"solution":{"explanation":["`hanoi(k, source, target, spare)` moves the top `k - 1` disks out of the way, moves the biggest disk, and moves the `k - 1` disks back on top of it. The roles of the rods swap between the two recursive calls. The total number of moves is `2^n - 1`, printed first."],"complexity":"Time O(2^n), space O(n) for the call stack.","code":{"python":"def hanoi(k, source, target, spare):\n    if k == 0:\n        return\n    hanoi(k - 1, source, spare, target)\n    print(source, target)\n    hanoi(k - 1, spare, target, source)\n\nn = int(input())\n\nprint(2 ** n - 1)\nhanoi(n, 1, 3, 2)\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid hanoi(int k, int source, int target, int spare) {\n    if (k == 0) {\n        return;\n    }\n    hanoi(k - 1, source, spare, target);\n    cout << source << \" \" << target << \"\\n\";\n    hanoi(k - 1, spare, target, source);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    cout << (1LL << n) - 1 << \"\\n\";\n    hanoi(n, 1, 3, 2);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void hanoi(int k, int source, int target, int spare) {\n        if (k == 0) {\n            return;\n        }\n        hanoi(k - 1, source, spare, target);\n        System.out.println(source + \" \" + target);\n        hanoi(k - 1, spare, target, source);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        System.out.println((1L << n) - 1);\n        hanoi((int) n, 1, 3, 2);\n    }\n}\n"}},"tests":[{"input":"2\n","output":"3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n"},{"input":"1\n","output":"1\n1 3\n"},{"input":"3\n","output":"7\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n"},{"input":"4\n","output":"15\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n1 2\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n2 3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n"},{"input":"5\n","output":"31\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n3 1\n2 1\n3 2\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n2 1\n3 2\n3 1\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n"},{"input":"6\n","output":"63\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n1 2\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n2 3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n1 2\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n3 1\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n1 2\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n2 3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n3 1\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n2 3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n1 2\n3 1\n3 2\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n2 1\n3 1\n2 3\n1 2\n1 3\n2 3\n"},{"input":"7\n","output":"127\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n3 1\n2 1\n3 2\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n2 1\n3 2\n3 1\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2\n1 3\n2 1\n2 3\n1 3\n1 2\n3 2